WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.064
Imagine the evening coordinator has thirty seconds to

00:00:03.064 --> 00:00:06.128
tell tomorrow's crew what changed. Before choosing an

00:00:06.128 --> 00:00:09.192
AI model, write one handoff you would trust.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.739
Read these three lines. Jo owns the crate

00:00:12.739 --> 00:00:15.718
pickup. The key has a location. The driver

00:00:15.718 --> 00:00:18.697
is still unconfirmed. Pause here and write the

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:21.304
handoff you would give the morning crew.

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:24.572
Here is one reference answer. It keeps the

00:00:24.572 --> 00:00:27.290
owner and deadline, points back to the source

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.008
lines, and leaves the open question open. Save

00:00:30.008 --> 00:00:32.726
your version before looking at a model draft.

00:00:33.294 --> 00:00:36.283
Decide now what earns a pass. Could someone

00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:39.272
act on the deadline? Can you trace every

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:42.260
statement? Would an unknown assignment still look unknown?

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.502
These checks work with any model.

00:00:45.054 --> 00:00:48.222
Score this deliberately flawed draft. It sounds efficient:

00:00:48.222 --> 00:00:51.390
Jo drives Route B at nine. But that

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.558
sentence joined two unrelated facts. Someone reading it

00:00:54.558 --> 00:00:56.934
might stop looking for a driver.

00:00:57.494 --> 00:01:00.769
Mark the exact claim that failed. Keep the

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:04.045
original notes, reference answer, rubric, draft and decision

00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:07.320
together. When you run a real model, add

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.595
its version, elapsed time and the edits the

00:01:10.595 --> 00:01:11.414
coordinator made.

00:01:11.974 --> 00:01:14.334
Here’s the rule to take with you. A

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:16.693
handoff is ready when the next person can

00:01:16.693 --> 00:01:19.053
act on it and trace each claim to

00:01:19.053 --> 00:01:21.412
the notes. If nobody has a driver yet,

00:01:21.412 --> 00:01:23.182
the handoff should still say so.

00:01:23.734 --> 00:01:26.796
Your turn. Write three fictional shift notes and

00:01:26.796 --> 00:01:29.858
leave one assignment open. Write your own handoff

00:01:29.858 --> 00:01:32.920
first. Then give the notes to a model

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.983
and score its reply. Did it preserve the

00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:38.662
gap? Keep both answers and your score.

00:01:39.214 --> 00:01:41.929
You now have a baseline: one small example

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:44.645
you can trust. Bring it with you as

00:01:44.645 --> 00:01:47.360
the app grows. When you change a prompt

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.076
or try another model, run those notes again

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.791
and see what changed. See you in the

00:01:52.791 --> 00:01:53.470
next lab.
